# nr-tui-launch-ledger: the process lifecycle ledger in mixdog-launch (src/lifecycle.rs and src/lifecycle/).
# Nothing here could be compiled (no cargo in this task): the dependency edges below are what the sources use.

## 1. native/core/crates/mixdog-launch/Cargo.toml
[dependencies], beside mixdog-store (the ledger's file lock is mixdog-statefile's hold_lock, the one implementation of Node's withFileLock):
    mixdog-statefile = { workspace = true }

Add a Windows target section (after the existing [target.'cfg(unix)'.dependencies]); the parent pid (process snapshot) and the working set
(GetProcessMemoryInfo) of OsHost, the same two queries the core's crash_record.rs makes:
    [target.'cfg(windows)'.dependencies]
    windows-sys = { workspace = true, features = ["Win32_Foundation", "Win32_System_Diagnostics_ToolHelp", "Win32_System_ProcessStatus", "Win32_System_Threading"] }

libc (unix: getppid, sysconf) is already a unix dependency of the crate. [dev-dependencies] need nothing new (tempfile, tokio with rt and
macros and time, mixdog-testkit are there).

## 2. native/core/Cargo.lock
Regenerate (cargo update --workspace --offline): package mixdog-launch gains the dependencies "mixdog-statefile" and "windows-sys". Both are
in the lock already; no package is added.

## 3. native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace-hack/Cargo.toml
cargo hakari generate: windows-sys gains the four features above in the hack's Windows section if it does not have them.

## 4. D:\mixdog-build\tools\unify-test-features.py
Run it after (1): mixdog-launch's tests build mixdog-statefile (normal dependency now), which has no test-only features the launch tests use.

## 5. compose_flip.rs SERVICE_PENDING
Nothing: the crate registers no protocol method.

## 6. Where the files are (a note for the binary tasks that call this)
The ledger is `<data>/diagnostics/process-lifecycle.jsonl` with the markers in `<data>/diagnostics/process-lifecycle.active/`, as Node's
process-lifecycle.mjs keeps them (its `paths()` joins resolvePluginData() with 'diagnostics'); the task text names `<data>/process-lifecycle`,
which is not what Node writes, and the files are kept identical. The entry points the binary calls are unchanged in name and arguments:
begin_process_lifecycle(role), record_catchable_fatal(&handle, text), finish_process_lifecycle(handle, reason, code); added
finish_process_lifecycle_async(handle, reason, code). LifecycleEnd gained `recorded: bool`, and its `reason` is now the strongest reason the
run reported (Node's rule), not the one the caller passed. The binary ends a failing process with finish_process_lifecycle (a try-lock
append, safe where nothing may wait) and ends a normal run on an executor with finish_process_lifecycle_async.
